<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/advanced/fluid-dynamics">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson is mechanics grown up. It assumes the conservation of energy from <em>Energy and Its
    Conservation</em>, Newton's second law from <em>Newton's Three Laws</em>, and the kinematics of
    <em>Motion in One Dimension</em> — and it extends the momentum bookkeeping of <em>Momentum &amp;
    Collisions</em> to fluids, with <em>Thermal Physics</em>'s friction becoming viscosity.
    Nothing here needs new mathematics beyond squares and ratios; the new skill is tracking fields instead of
    objects.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>use continuity as bookkeeping: predict how speed changes when a pipe's area changes, and explain why area falls as radius squared;</li>
    <li>apply Bernoulli to a Venturi neck or a leaking tank, and state its fine-print conditions;</li>
    <li>explain how deliberate roughness — golf-ball dimples, vortex generators — cuts drag by tripping the boundary layer turbulent;</li>
    <li>say what the Navier–Stokes equations are, and why turbulence remains an open problem.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Fluids are just matter that flows, but they break the habits you built in mechanics:
    there is no "the object" any more, only a continuum of parcels pushing on each other.
    The advanced move is to stop tracking particles and track <em>fields</em> — velocity,
    pressure and density as functions of position and time. Almost everything then
    follows from two conservation laws, both expressible in one line each. <strong>Mass
    conservation (continuity):</strong> what flows into a pipe must flow out, so where
    the pipe narrows the fluid must speed up. <strong>Energy conservation
    (Bernoulli):</strong> along a smooth, steady flow, faster movement is paid for by
    lower pressure. Between them they explain flight, plumbing, blood flow, carburettors,
    sailing and why the shower curtain attacks you. What they cannot explain — turbulence,
    the wild regime every real high-speed flow eventually enters — is the subject's live
    frontier and one of mathematics' famous open problems.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more piece of vocabulary does heavy lifting: <strong>pressure</strong> is force
    per area, and in a static fluid it increases with depth as
    <strong>P = P₀ + ρgh</strong> — every 10 m of water adds roughly one atmosphere
    (1000 × 9.8 × 10 ≈ 98 kPa). Hydrostatic pressure is the baseline on which all the
    dynamics of this page is written.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="continuity at work — the same 10 L/s flow through pipes of different radius: halve the radius, quadruple the speed (area falls as r²)" widget="plotter" config={contCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the curve plots flow speed against pipe radius for one fixed flow — 10 litres
    per second. Near the right edge a 20 cm pipe ambles at about 0.08 m/s; at a 2 cm radius the same flow runs at
    8 m/s. Trace it and the speed climbs as one over radius squared — the graphical face of "area falls as r²" —
    with no forces anywhere: pure bookkeeping, water neither piling up nor vanishing.
  </p>

  <p>
    Read the figure concretely: 10 litres per second through a 20 cm-radius pipe moves at
    0.01/(π × 0.2²) ≈ 0.08 m/s — a lazy stroll. Neck the same flow down to a 2 cm radius
    and continuity demands 8 m/s — a hundredfold speed-up from a tenfold squeeze, because
    area falls as r². This is garden-hose physics (thumb over the end), river-gorge
    physics (rivers accelerate through narrows and drop sediment when they widen again),
    and nozzle physics — and it is pure bookkeeping: no forces needed, only the fact that
    water does not pile up or vanish.
  </p>

  <h2>Bernoulli's principle: speed buys pressure</h2>
  <p>
    Where fluid speeds up, its pressure drops, by exactly the kinetic energy per volume
    it gained: <strong>P + ½ρv² + ρgh = constant</strong> along a streamline (for smooth,
    steady, non-viscous flow — each condition matters, see below). The full worked
    example, including the machine-building payoff:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="a Venturi throat, quantitatively — continuity sets the speed, Bernoulli sets the pressure" widget="equationStep" config={bernCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the steps run the two conservation laws in sequence — continuity first fixes
    the neck's speed (a quarter of the area, four times the speed), then Bernoulli prices that speed in pressure:
    30 kPa below the wide section. Watch the division of labour: continuity fixes the speed, Bernoulli prices it.
    Every machine on the last card is this two-step, running.
  </p>

  <p>
    A beautiful limiting case: how fast does water spurt from a hole 5 m below the
    surface of a tank? Bernoulli between the surface (v ≈ 0, pressure P₀) and the jet
    (pressure P₀, speed v, dropped h) gives <strong>v = √(2gh)</strong> — Torricelli's
    law, identical to free-fall from height h. Here v = √(2 × 9.8 × 5) ≈ 9.9 m/s. The
    pressure of the water column has been converted entirely into directed motion, with
    no memory of the tank's width — another conservation law giving an answer without
    forces.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "lift is because air must travel further over the curved
    top and therefore faster, by equal transit time." The equal-transit-time claim is
    false — nothing obliges the flows to reunite (they demonstrably do not; upper-surface
    air arrives <em>early</em>), and flat plates and upside-down aerofoils fly fine. The
    honest account combines two equivalent views: Newton's — the wing deflects a large
    mass of air downward each second, and the air pushes back up (lift as F = ma on air);
    and Bernoulli's — the deflection and circulation establish a pressure difference
    between surfaces, whose integral <em>is</em> the same lift. Both are correct and
    measurable; "fast air sucks" is only a slogan for the second.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> speed is bought with pressure along a streamline, and Torricelli's jet shows the
    accounting closing exactly. Real fluids, though, stick — and sticking changes everything.
  </p>

  <h2>Viscosity and the Reynolds number: one number to rule the flow</h2>
  <p>
    Real fluids stick — to themselves and to walls. <strong>Viscosity</strong> μ measures
    the internal friction: honey 10⁴× water; air, surprisingly, only 1/50th of water.
    Viscosity makes the <strong>no-slip condition</strong> universal: fluid in contact
    with a surface moves <em>with</em> it, so every real flow has a <strong>boundary
    layer</strong>, a thin region where speed ramps from zero to the free-stream value.
    All of aerodynamic drag lives in what that layer does — stay attached (smooth, cheap
    flow) or separate into eddies (a wide, expensive wake). The single dimensionless
    number deciding which regime you are in is the <strong>Reynolds number</strong>,
    Re = ρvL/μ — inertia divided by viscosity, a ratio of the two timescales of the
    flow. Below about 10³ (for pipe flow) flow is <strong>laminar</strong>: orderly
    layers, momentum exchanged by friction. Above a few thousand it becomes
    <strong>turbulent</strong>: chaotic eddies at every scale, mixing ferociously.
  </p>
  <p>
    The striking fact is how vast the range is. A swimming bacterium (1 μm-scale, ~30
    μm/s) lives at Re ≈ 10⁻⁴, where inertia is irrelevant and water feels like tar —
    stop whipping your flagella and you halt within microseconds; swimming at that scale
    means rowing in a world with no coasting. A whale lives at Re ≈ 10⁸, pure inertia,
    where turbulence is the default and streamlining is everything. Human engineering
    spans the same range: blood in capillaries flows at Re ≈ 0.001 (laminar, cells in
    single file), while a car at highway speed sits at Re ≈ 4 × 10⁶ (turbulent boundary
    layer — which is actually an advantage, see the dimples below).
  </p>
  <p>
    In the gentle laminar regime the drag law is Stokes': F = 6πηrv, linear in speed —
    which sets the crawl of very small particles through fluid. A cloud droplet of 10 μm
    radius sinks at v = 2r²(ρ_drop)g/(9η_air) ≈ 1 cm/s — clouds stay aloft because their
    drops are Stokes-slow. By drizzle size (1 mm diameter) the flow around the drop has
    Re ≈ 250, quadratic drag has taken over, and terminal velocity is about 4 m/s; the
    largest raindrops reach roughly 9 m/s before surface tension shatters them. In pipes,
    laminar flow
    obeys <strong>Poiseuille's law</strong>, Q = πr⁴ΔP/(8μL), and the fourth power is a
    medical emergency in waiting: an artery narrowed from 3 mm to 2 mm radius carries
    (2/3)⁴ ≈ 0.20 of its former flow at the same pressure — an 80% loss. The body
    compensates with pressure (hypertension), which is the cardiovascular system paying
    interest on geometry.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "viscosity is a minor correction — honey is sticky, water is not, so real
  flows are basically frictionless." It seems reasonable — water's viscosity is tiny in everyday units. But the
  section's scale pairs show the opposite: at Re ≈ 10⁻⁴ water feels like tar to a swimming bacterium, and blood
  in capillaries flows in single file. The correct picture: what matters is inertia's ratio to viscosity — the
  Reynolds number — not viscosity's absolute size: the same water is honey to a swimmer one micrometre long and
  nearly ideal to a whale.</p>

  <h2>Drag, dimples and delay: engineering the boundary layer</h2>
  <p>
    At high Reynolds numbers drag has two parts: <strong>pressure drag</strong> from the
    low-pressure wake behind a blunt body, and <strong>skin friction</strong> in the
    boundary layer. The counter-intuitive discovery of the 20th century: a turbulent
    boundary layer, though more frictional, hugs the surface better — turbulence pumps
    fast fluid down to the wall, delaying separation, shrinking the wake and cutting
    pressure drag by far more than the friction costs. This is exactly why golf balls
    have <strong>dimples</strong>: they trip the boundary layer turbulent, and a dimpled
    ball carries about 30% further than a smooth one at the same drive. Aircraft wings
    use vortex generators, cyclists wear rough skinsuits, and sharks' denticles do it
    passively — all tripping devices on the same principle. The design art of aerodynamics
    is largely the placement of deliberate roughness.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "smooth surfaces always have less drag — polish everything." It seems
  reasonable because roughness obviously adds skin friction. But at high Reynolds numbers the larger cost is
  pressure drag from the low-pressure wake: a dimpled golf ball carries about 30% further than a smooth one at
  the same drive, because a tripped-turbulent layer delays separation and shrinks the wake. The correct picture:
  a turbulent layer costs a little skin friction and saves far more in pressure drag — which is why vortex
  generators, rough skinsuits and sharks' denticles all exist.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> viscosity builds a boundary layer, the Reynolds number decides its fate, and
    deliberate roughness exploits turbulence. The next section names the equations underneath — and the
    million-dollar prize for taming them.
  </p>

  <h2>Turbulence and Navier–Stokes: the open frontier</h2>
  <p>
    Write Newton's second law for a fluid parcel — acceleration plus advective rate of
    change equals pressure gradient, viscous and body forces — and you get the
    <strong>Navier–Stokes equations</strong>, formulated (for special cases) in the
    1820s–40s. They describe everything from coffee stirring to galactic gas, and they
    are <em>exact</em>: no quantum corrections needed at human scales. But they are
    non-linear — the fluid carries itself along — and that non-linearity breeds
    turbulence: motion at every scale, largest eddies feeding smallest (Kolmogorov's
    1941 cascade, energy flowing to dissipation at ~kT per molecule of scale), with no
    closed-form solutions and no small approximations that survive. The Clay Mathematics
    Institute's Millennium Prize — one million dollars — asks for a proof that
    three-dimensional Navier–Stokes solutions always remain smooth (or an example that
    develops a singularity). It has been open since 2000, and the smart money says the
    answer requires mathematics not yet invented. Meanwhile engineers march on:
    computational fluid dynamics discretises the equations onto millions of cells;
    aircraft are designed and weather is forecast (the differential-equations lesson's
    chaos caveat applies) by numerically solving the same equations Descartes-era
    geometry started.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A river narrows to a quarter of its cross-sectional area in a gorge. What happens to the water's speed,
    and which law says so — force bookkeeping or motion bookkeeping?</li>
    <li>An artery's radius falls from 3 mm to 1.5 mm at constant pressure. Roughly what fraction of its former
    flow survives, and why is the body's response dangerous?</li>
    <li>Why does a smooth sphere suddenly lose most of its drag near Re ≈ 10⁵, and how did golf-ball engineers
    turn that discovery into distance?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The speed quadruples: continuity requires the flow rate to stay constant, so
    quartering the area demands four times the speed — pure bookkeeping. (2) Poiseuille's fourth power: halving
    the radius multiplies the flow by (1/2)⁴ = 1/16, so roughly 6% survives. The body compensates by raising
    pressure — hypertension as interest paid on geometry. (3) Near that Reynolds number the boundary layer trips
    turbulent on a smooth sphere, hugging the surface longer and dropping drag by a factor of 4–5 — the drag
    crisis. Dimples do this deliberately: a dimpled ball flies about 30% further at the same drive.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Fluids are continua: track fields — velocity, pressure, density — not particles.</li>
    <li>Continuity is bookkeeping: where area falls, speed rises, and area falls as radius squared.</li>
    <li>Bernoulli P + ½ρv² + ρgh = constant: along a smooth, steady, non-viscous streamline, speed is paid for in pressure.</li>
    <li>The Reynolds number Re = ρvL/μ — inertia over viscosity — decides laminar versus turbulent, from bacteria to whales.</li>
    <li>Deliberate roughness trips the boundary layer turbulent and cuts pressure drag: dimples, vortex generators, skinsuits, denticles.</li>
    <li>Poiseuille's fourth power makes small narrowings medical emergencies: (2/3)⁴ leaves a fifth of the flow.</li>
    <li>Navier–Stokes are exact and unsolved in general — turbulence is classical physics' open frontier.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Statistical Mechanics</em> is the natural continuation: it builds the microscopic account of pressure and
    temperature that this lesson borrowed. <em>Special Relativity</em> and <em>Quantum
    Foundations</em> lie further along the same advanced ladder — and notice that fluids stayed classical: the
    hardest problem here needs no quantum corrections at human scales, only mathematics not yet invented.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Buoyancy: hydrostatics paying dividends.</strong> Archimedes' principle — the
    upward force on a submerged body equals the weight of fluid displaced — falls straight
    out of P = P₀ + ρgh: the pressure on the bottom face exceeds that on the top by
    ρg·h·A, exactly the displaced fluid's weight. It floats when its density is lower.
    Ice (917 kg/m³ in water, 1000) floats with 91.7% submerged — the iceberg rule of
    thumb ("nine-tenths below") is a density ratio, not a myth. The same pressure
    imbalance drives convection: warm air is less dense, buoyancy lifts it, and the
    atmosphere's entire vertical heat transport — thermals, thunderstorms, the Hadley
    cells the meteorology pages discuss — is buoyancy turning hydrostatic pressure into
    motion at planetary scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>When Bernoulli breaks.</strong> The three fine-print conditions are each
    physics. <em>Non-viscous:</em> in a long pipe, viscosity bleeds energy as friction, so
    pressure drops along the flow even at constant speed — that is Poiseuille's loss, and
    it is why water towers exist. <em>Steady:</em> accelerating flows (start-up, waves,
    pulsatile blood) need unsteady terms; the heart's flow reverses the usual logic
    twice a second. <em>Along a streamline:</em> across streamlines, in a swirling flow,
    pressure varies with curvature (the bath-plug vortex: low pressure at the core).
    Treating "fast means low pressure" as an unconditional law produces real errors —
    the classic one is explaining a spinning ball's curveball break entirely by surface
    speed, when the deflection is set by asymmetric separation and circulation
    (the Magnus effect) — direction right, bookkeeping wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Quantitative drag across regimes.</strong> The drag coefficient plot against
    Reynolds number is one of engineering's most instructive curves: at low Re,
    C_d ∝ 1/Re (Stokes), so drag is linear in v; at high Re, C_d flattens to ≈ 0.3–1.5
    for blunt bodies, drag quadratic in v — hence F = ½ρC_dAv², the formula behind
    terminal velocity, wind loads and fuel economy. The famous <em>drag crisis</em>: near
    Re ≈ 10⁵ the boundary layer trips turbulent on a smooth sphere, the wake narrows and
    C_d drops by a factor of 4–5 — the reason a cricket ball swung with one rough and one
    smooth hemisphere misbehaves, and why cyclists shave legs but wear rough suits.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Lift, quantitatively.</strong> A Boeing 747 (wing area ~510 m²) cruises at
    250 m/s where dynamic pressure ½ρv² ≈ 37 kPa; a lift coefficient of about 0.5 gives
    L = ½ρv²C_L·A ≈ 9.4 MN — right for its 400-tonne weight. The numbers close, which is
    the quiet satisfaction of the subject: deflection, circulation and pressure
    integration are three measurements of one momentum transfer. The same arithmetic in
    reverse sizes wind turbines (Betz limit: no turbine extracts more than 16/27 ≈ 59.3%
    of the wind's power — a hard bound from continuity and momentum, not materials),
    sailing and hydrofoils.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open research.</strong> Beyond the Navier–Stokes existence problem: no
    predictive first-principles theory of fully developed turbulence exists (statistical
    closure theories fail; supercomputers reach Re ~10⁴–10⁵ only); the transition to
    turbulence is still mapped empirically; and Lagrangian turbulence — where a fluid
    parcel actually goes, the mathematics of chaotic mixing — governs everything from
    plankton dispersal to pollutant spread and remains an active field. Fluid dynamics
    is classical physics with its hardest problems still on the table.
  </p>

